From 36ba3d4df504cc6dd12f9c3b732dd44527154772 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 08:17:57 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] psbt: refuse a version conversion we would later abort on

psbt_set_version reports whether libwally accepted the change, and callers
treat that as meaning the PSBT is now usable in the new version. It does not
mean that. wally_psbt_set_version can succeed on a PSBT that
wally_psbt_get_length then rejects, and the next thing anybody does with a
converted PSBT is store it or send it, both of which go through
psbt_get_bytes, which calls abort().

A coinbase transaction is such a PSBT: a null previous outpoint has no
version 2 representation. fundchannel_complete converts one, every check it
makes passes, the peer is told the channel exists, and then the channel is
saved and lightningd dies with SIGABRT. Reachable by anyone who can reach the
RPC, which is not the same as reachable by a peer, so this is robustness
rather than security, but it is a live abort and there are ten conversion
sites rather than one.

validate_psbt already asks exactly the right question without aborting, and
every caller of psbt_set_version already handles false. So ask it here and
let them.
---
 bitcoin/psbt.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/bitcoin/psbt.c b/bitcoin/psbt.c
index 10dada97e..2ad4224b9 100644
--- a/bitcoin/psbt.c
+++ b/bitcoin/psbt.c
@@ -1116,5 +1116,16 @@ bool psbt_set_version(struct wally_psbt *psbt, u32 version)
 		ok &= wally_psbt_set_tx_modifiable_flags(psbt, WALLY_PSBT_TXMOD_INPUTS | WALLY_PSBT_TXMOD_OUTPUTS) == WALLY_OK;
 	}
 	tal_wally_end(psbt);
+
+	/* Setting the version can succeed on a PSBT that cannot then be
+	 * serialized in it, and a caller has no reason to expect that: the
+	 * next thing anybody does with a converted PSBT is store it or send
+	 * it, and psbt_get_bytes aborts rather than failing. A coinbase
+	 * transaction is such a PSBT, since a null previous outpoint has no
+	 * version 2 representation. Report it here, where every caller
+	 * already handles a false. */
+	if (ok && !validate_psbt(psbt))
+		return false;
+
 	return ok;
 }
